 val="0"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2800"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buChar char="•"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2800" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>Impairs neurological development leading to intellectual deficits</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p><a:p><a:pPr indent="-342900" lvl="0" marL="342900" marR="0" rtl="0" algn="l"><a:lnSpc><a:spcPct val="100000"/></a:lnSpc><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2800"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buChar char="•"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2800" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>Controlled by limiting dietary intake of Phe</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p></p:txBody></p:sp><p:pic><p:nvPicPr><p:cNvPr descr="An image depicting the alternative pathways for catabolism of phenylalanine in phenylketonuria. At the top is phenylalanine, which begins with a hexagon with alternation double-bonded sides, which bonds right to CH2, which bonds right to CH. CH bonds up to NH3 (plus sign over N in a pink box) and right to COO-. Below this is an arrow pointing down, identified as aminotransferase (in blue). To the right is a curved arrow pointing from pyruvate to alanine. In the middle of the arc is PLP. Pyruvate begins with C, which bonds left to CH3, double bonds down to O, and bonds right to COO-. Alanine begins with CH, which bonds left to CH3, up to NH3 (plus sign over N and in a pink box), and right to COO-. Below the arrow from phenylalanine is phenylpyruvate, which begins with C double bonding up to O, bonding right to COO-, and binding left to CH2. CH2 bonds left to a hexagon with alternating double-bonded sides. Below this are two arrows, with one pointing down and right to phenyllactate and the other pointing down and left to phenylacetate. On the right arrow is a curved arrow pointing from NADH [plus] H+ to NAD+. On the left arrow, another arrow jets out to CO2. Below this H2O joins the arrow pointing down and left. Phenylacetate begins with a hexagon with alternating double-bonded sides, which bonds right to CH2, which bonds right to COO-. Phenyllactate begins with a hexagon with alternating double-bonded sides, which bonds right to CH2, which bonds right to CH. CH bonds up to OH and right to COO-." id="384" name="Google Shape;384;p51"/><p:cNvPicPr preferRelativeResize="0"/><p:nvPr/></p:nvPicPr><p:blipFill rotWithShape="1"><a:blip r:embed="rId3"><a:alphaModFix/></a:blip><a:srcRect b="0" l="0" r="0" t="0"/><a:stretch/></p:blipFill><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="5105400" y="1598612"/><a:ext cx="3429000" cy="5097462"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="rect"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln><a:noFill/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:pic></p:spTree></p:cSld><p:clrMapOvr><a:masterClrMapping/></p:clrMapOvr></p:sld>